TY  - CONF
TI  - Automated Requirements Assessment and UML Generation from Natural Language
AU  - A. Zhumagaliyev
AU  - H. -j. Kim
T2  - 2026 IEEE/ACIS 24th International Conference on Software Engineering Research, Management and Applications (SERA)
AB  - Large Language Models increasingly generate UML diagrams from natural language requirements, yet current approaches produce diagrams with systematic errors: hallucinated attributes, over-engineered structures, and anti-pattern violations. Our analysis of ChatGPT reveals these are predictable patterns, not random failures, stemming from two root causes: (1) generation uses unconstrained prompts without methodology-specific guidance, and (2) outputs lack grounding in established diagram notation. We present a two-stage approach addressing both issues. First, a three-layer validation pipeline assesses requirements across eight quality dimensions, providing actionable improvement hints to users. Second, a generator with Retrieval-Augmented Generation retrieves patterns from official PlantUML documentation, using principled prompts grounded in Larman's and Cockburn's UML methodology. Evaluation across 15 test cases with 75 generation runs demonstrates 100% class diagram usability and 96% use case diagram usability versus 87% and 72% for ChatGPT respectively. However, ChatGPT exhibits systematic semantic errors: 11 hallucinated class attributes versus zero for our system, and 13 anti-pattern violations versus one. Multi-run analysis confirms 93.3% structural consistency (95% Wilson CI: [70%, 99%]) with 0% hallucination rate (95% Wilson CI: [0%, 4%]). Notably, ablation study reveals that well-engineered prompts incorporating Larman's mapmaker principle are the primary driver of quality improvement for established diagram types, with RAG providing future-proofing for newly introduced PlantUML constructs (e.g., C4 architecture diagrams) outside the model's training data. Our results demonstrate that methodology-grounded prompt engineering on a less capable model (GPT-4o) prevents attribute hallucination that persists in a more capable model (GPT-5.2 Thinking) without such constraints. The deployed system at umlreq.com enables iterative validate-then-generate workflows.
