TI  - A Comparative Study of Large Language Models for Model-Driven Reverse Engineering
AU  - H. Abdelmalek
AU  - Z. Babaalla
AU  - A. Jakimi
AU  - R. Saadane
T2  - 2026 IEEE 13th International Symposium on Signal, Image, Video and Communications (ISIVC)
AB  - The extraction of abstract models from the source code is one of the main focuses of model-driven reverse engineering and is particularly important for legacy systems, which do not have updated documentation. Recovering abstract models from legacy systems is complex, given that the technical constructs combined with domain-level constructs are implemented through multiple layers of technology and system-specific rules. Current static analysis and heuristic-based approaches can struggle to separate these concerns. The recent emergence of large language models (LLMs) is promising with regard to modeling software, but behaviors and expected outcomes related to LLMs when performing reverse engineering tasks are challenging. This paper compares the performance of various LLMs on a reverse engineering task related to the recovery of abstract models from the source code. This comparison aims to identify the domain constructs represented through an intermediate representation of the source code. Three LLMs, namely ChatGPT, Claude, and Meta AI, were assessed on the same input representation, with each LLM performing the task of recovering abstract models through the use of a common prompt. Evaluation of the three LLM outputs was performed on a common open-source C\# project, which serves as the target for the LLM outputs to be evaluated against an established reference Platform-Independent Model. Evaluation of model outputs was performed according to precision, recall, and F1 score, measuring individual categories of model elements.
