AB  - Validating end-to-end business processes requires strict adherence to state logic, particularly for long-horizon workflows involving complex multi-role interactions. While Large Language Models (LLMs) offer promising automation capabilities, current approaches often lack integration with formal testing principles, leading to inherent “happy path bias” and difficulties in maintaining context across extended sessions. This paper proposes a hybrid, business process-centric methodology that addresses these limitations through a “separation of concerns” architecture. Our framework utilizes LLMs for semantic comprehension while rigorously enforcing Specification-Based Testing techniques-specifically Equivalence Partitioning, Boundary Value Analysis (BVA), and Pairwise Testing-to optimize data coverage. Furthermore, the methodology employs graph-based modeling for deterministic navigation and systematically synthesizes exception flows to ensure robustness for cross-functional user journeys. Crucially, the generated test artifacts are engineered for compliance with the ISO/IEC/IEEE 29119-3 standard, ensuring industrial interoperability. Empirical evaluation across multiple subject systems demonstrates high-fidelity performance, achieving significant Boundary Value Coverage (avg. >88%), superior State Transition Coverage (avg. >95%), and high semantic quality scores (>92%). Moreover, an economic feasibility analysis confirms the framework’s efficiency, with operational costs ranging from  ${\$}$ 1.41 to  ${\$}$ 1.82 per complete regression suite. These findings suggest that integrating LLMs as semantic engines within a framework grounded in established software testing techniques offers a robust and cost-effective path toward scalable test automation.
