TI  - Improving Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Medical Literature: A Case Study on Pertussis Research
AU  - H. Takabatake
AU  - N. P. Martono
AU  - A. Kuwae
AU  - T. Iuchi
AU  - H. Ohwada
T2  - 2025 15th International Conference on Information & Communication Technology and System (ICTS)
AB  - Integrating knowledge from scientific literature is essential for advancing medical research, including drug discovery and pathological research. However, as the relevant sources span multiple fields—including molecular biology, bacteriology, and infectious diseases—and the number of scientific literatures in each field continues to increase every year, it has become increasingly difficult for researchers to comprehensively extract the knowledge they need. To address this challenge, we propose a retrieval system designed for retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) that supports effective knowledge integration from scientific literature. Our approach introduces a two-stage retrieval pipeline: an initial hard filtering step based on domain-specific keywords, and a hybrid search with a reranker as the second stage. As a case study, we constructed a pertussis-related dataset inspired by PubMedQA and evaluated our method under a multi-paper task setting, which better reflects the real-world challenges of scientific knowledge integration. Experimental results demonstrate that our method substantially outperforms existing approaches such as HyDE, RAG-Fusion, and a hybrid search, achieving improvements of 13.4 to 20.4 points in MAP@10. However, we also observed a decrease in MRR@10 compared with settings without hard filtering in the first stage of the retrieval pipeline, as relevant passages were sometimes entirely excluded from the top-10 results. These findings suggest that while our approach contributes to the overall effectiveness, the strength of hard filtering must be carefully calibrated to avoid excluding relevant passages. Furthermore, in the evaluation of the overall RAG pipeline, including downstream tasks, our approach consistently achieved the highest scores across multiple LLMs, including GPT-4.1, Claude Sonnet 4, and Gemini 2.5 Pro. This demonstrates that the improvements extend beyond retrieval, benefiting downstream inference tasks and showing robustness across different models.
