TY  - CONF
TI  - Semantic Consistency Analysis of Large Language Models for Rare Disease Diagnosis Using Clinical Ontologies
AU  - D. Gujjula
AU  - S. Vairachilai
AU  - S. Velagapudi
T2  - 2026 2nd International Conference on Intelligent Systems and Computational Networks (ICISCN)
AB  - Rare disease diagnosis remains challenging due to heterogeneous symptom patterns, limited clinical expertise, and the scarcity of structured biomedical knowledge. Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated promising capabilities in generating diagnostic reasoning from clinical narratives; however, these models frequently produce hallucinated or semantically inconsistent medical associations. To address this limitation, this paper proposes an ontology-guided semantic validation framework for auditing LLM-generated diagnostic explanations. The framework extracts medical entities from generated outputs and maps them to standardized biomedical ontologies, including UMLS, SNOMED CT, Orphanet, and the Human Phenotype Ontology (HPO). Ontology-based knowledge graph validation is then applied to verify disease–phenotype relationships and detect unsupported associations. Experimental evaluation on 500 rare disease clinical case narratives demonstrates that the proposed framework improves concept grounding from 78.4% to 89.2%, increases relation validity from 72.1% to 86.5%, and reduces hallucinated diagnostic associations from 18.6% to 7.4%. These results highlight that integrating structured biomedical knowledge with generative language models significantly enhances the reliability, interpretability, and semantic consistency of AI-assisted rare disease diagnosis.
