TI  - SymptoMatch: Improving Clinical Symptom Recognition via Bidirectional Learning From Layman Expression
AU  - C. Kim
AU  - J. Seo
AU  - C. Kim
AU  - W. H. Kim
AU  - S. J. Kim
AU  - E. Ko
AU  - J. Kim
T2  - IEEE Access
AB  - Interpreting diverse layperson symptom descriptions poses a significant challenge in medical information retrieval, especially when user queries lack clinical terminology or contain ambiguous expressions. We present SymptoMatch, a deep retrieval model that aligns non-standard symptom expressions with precise medical terms. Based on KLUE-RoBERTa-Large, SymptoMatch was fine-tuned using 62,406 samples from symptom-complaint pairs derived from Electronic Medical Records (EMR), Unified Medical Language System (UMLS) symptom codes, and paraphrases generated by Large Language Models (LLMs). We introduce a bidirectional learning framework incorporating Multiple Negatives Symmetric Ranking (MNSR) Loss and in-batch negative sampling, enabling robust query $\to $ term and term $\to $ query semantic mappings. The model was evaluated using an expert-validated dataset where emergency medicine specialists matched user queries to clinically appropriate symptoms. SymptoMatch substantially outperformed baseline embedding models and LLMs, achieving 81.48% Exact Match, 92.22% Recall@2, and 94.44% Recall@3. These results demonstrate the model’s effectiveness in bridging the semantic gap between patient-reported symptoms and standardized medical terminology, providing a practical solution for real-world medical information systems.
