TI  - Benchmarking NLP Paradigms for Livestream Transcript Highlight Detection
AU  - M. U. Farzan
AU  - D. N. Aswani
AU  - A. R. Ikram
T2  - 2025 13th International Japan-Africa Conference on Electronics, Communications, and Computations (JAC-ECC)
AB  - The rapid growth of livestreaming platforms has created an urgent need for systems that can automatically detect highlight moments, which are critical for content monetization, discoverability, and audience engagement. Existing approaches predominantly rely on audio-visual or multimodal pipelines, which are computationally expensive and difficult to scale. Transcript-based detection offers a lightweight alternative; however, progress in this direction has been limited by the lack of reliably labeled text-only datasets and the absence of benchmarks that compare modern NLP paradigms specifically for transcript-level highlight detection. Although our curated dataset of 4,150 labeled livestream transcript segments is moderate in size, it provides high-quality, manually reviewed labels suitable for controlled and reproducible evaluation.In this study, we conduct the first cross-paradigm benchmark for transcript-based highlight detection, evaluating seven models across three major families—BERT-like encoders, embeddingbased classifiers, and prompted large language models—under a unified experimental framework. Our results show that BERT-like encoders achieve the strongest predictive performance, embedding models offer the best efficiency-accuracy trade-off, and prompted LLMs exhibit severe performance degradation on informal transcripts compared to their reported capabilities on formal text. Unlike prior benchmarks evaluating paradigms on well-structured documents, our evaluation on informal livestream transcripts, characterized by fragmented speech, domain-specific slang, and context-dependent semantics, reveals that paradigm rankings established on formal text do not transfer to specialized deployment contexts. These findings demonstrate that paradigm selection must account for linguistic register and domain vocabulary, providing practitioners with domain-specific guidance for designing scalable, text-driven highlight detection systems in the real-world live-stream environment.
