AB  - <p>This chapter explores the fundamental principles, construction methods, and advanced techniques of knowledge graphs (KGs), which have emerged as powerful tools in the digital age for representing information in a structured, interconnected manner. The chapter begins by examining the basic building blocks of a KG, using the Marvel Universe KG as a hands‐on example. It then delves into practical aspects of graph construction, including schema design, data extraction techniques, and real‐world implementation workflows. The chapter further explores how to enrich the semantics and utility of KGs using graph algorithms, graph embeddings, and graph neural networks (GNNs). Finally, it examines the synergy between KGs and large language models (LLMs), enabling automated knowledge extraction, ontology learning, and hybrid systems. Practical exercises and case studies, such as the Wayfair Annotation Dataset (WANDS) product data in Neo4j, reinforce the concepts and illustrate how KGs can be operationalized in real‐world settings. The chapter concludes by demonstrating how KGs serve as a bridge between human‐readable knowledge and machine‐processable intelligence, particularly in the era of generative AI.</p>
